A B S T R A C T
Perovskite solar cells (PSCs) face efficiency and stability limitations from electron transport layer (ETL) defects 
and carrier interfacial recombination. In this manuscript, a partial hydrolysis oxidation doping strategy is 
designed by introducing ammonium persulfate (APS) into SnO2 ETL to simultaneously enhances device perfor­
mance and stability. Mechanistic investigations reveal that the reactive species (HSO4
−, H2O2, and NH4
+) 
generated during APS hydrolysis act synergistically: effectively passivating oxygen vacancies (VO) and Sn2+
defects on SnO2 surface, thereby optimizing its band structure and electron mobility; and significantly reducing 
Pb2+ and I−vacancy defects in the perovskite phase via coordination interaction, leading to improved crystal­
lization kinetics. The optimized devices achieve a champion power conversion efficiency (PCE) of 25.32 % with 
negligible hysteresis and retain 95.3 % initial efficiency after 1500 h ambient storage. This dual-passivation 
approach provides new insights for developing stable and high-efficiency PSCs.
