A R T I C L E I N F O
Keywords:
Perovskite solar cells
Bi-carboxylate additive
Defect modification
A B S T R A C T
Perovskite solar cells (PSCs) are an emerging photovoltaic technology, and defect passivation and interfacial 
energy modification of the perovskite and transport layers are important for improving their efficiency and 
stability. In this paper, we adopt a two-layer modification engineering strategy and propose the use of a bi- 
carboxylate additive, N-Hydroxy succinimide (NHS), introduced into the electron transport layer (ETL) and 
perovskite layer. This scheme effectively reduces the defect density of perovskite films and improves the film 
quality of ETL and perovskite, thus improving the performance and stability of PSCs. In addition, the intro­
duction of NHS improves the charge extraction efficiency of the device, and the short-circuit current density (Jsc) 
of the PSCs is effectively enhanced. The optimum photoelectric conversion efficiency (PCE) of the optimized 
devices was 23.23%, and the fill factor (FF) was 80.32% with no significant hysteresis.
