Inorganic semiconductor CsBi3I10 (CBI) emerges as one of the most promising candidates for lead-free
perovskite solar cells (PSCs) due to its low toxicity, high visible light absorption and excellent humidity
tolerance. However, poor film morphology and high crystal defects limit its practical application
in photovoltaics. Herein, hot-casting and cold-casting technologies are proposed respectively in the
fabrication process of CBI to regulate the film quality and photoelectric properties. Significant
diﬀerences appear in the crystallization process and film morphology of CBI layers, resulting in distinct
device performance. In contrast to the CBI film with low density and high thickness formed via the hot-
casting technology, the cold-casting accelerates the crystallization rate of CBI, achieving a dense and
smooth
film
with
