A R T I C L E I N F O
Keywords:
Perovskite solar cells
PbI2 inverse-opal skeletons
Solid-liquid reaction
Crystallization
A B S T R A C T
Constructing a porous lead iodide (PbI2) layer is indeed a highly effective strategy for achieving high-quality 
perovskite films. This approach leverages the unique properties of the porous PbI2 structure to facilitate the 
efficient conversion of PbI2 into perovskite crystals, which is crucial for optimizing the performance of perovskite 
photovoltaics. Here, we fabricated a PbI2 inverse-opal skeleton (PbI2 IO) using a template-assisted method and 
precisely tuned its pore size through O2 plasma etching technology. Time-dependent in situ UV–vis absorption 
spectra and photoluminescence (PL) spectroscopy results revealed that the PbI2 IO promoted the infiltration of 
the organic amine salt solution, improved the solid-liquid reaction, and enhanced perovskite crystal growth. As a 
result, the perovskite films derived from the PbI2 IO exhibited improved crystallinity and significantly reduced 
defect density. Benefiting from the high-quality perovskite film, the perovskite solar cells (PSCs) based on the 
PbI2 IO achieved a remarkable power conversion efficiency (PCE) of 24.93% and demonstrated excellent long- 
term stability under humid ambient air conditions.
1. Introduction
Perovskite solar cells (PSCs) hold significant potential in the photo­
voltaic field owing to their simple solution-based fabrication process 
[1], low cost [2,3], and high power conversion efficiencies (PCE) [4,5]. 
