A R T I C L E I N F O
Keywords:
π-conjugated
Defect passivation
Multiple sites
High efficiency
A B S T R A C T
In this study, two π-conjugated Lewis base molecules with high electron densities, 4-chlorothieno[2,3-d]py­
rimidine (4-ClThPM) and 4-chloroquinazoline (4-ClQZL), were incorporated into tin halide perovskite pre­
cursors, respectively. Both additives contain multiple functional sites that can interact with components of the tin 
perovskite, effectively inhibiting lattice distortion and reducing the defect density. Due to the electron-donating 
effect of the thiophene ring, the stronger coordination interactions between 4-ClThPM and SnI2 as well as the 
stronger hydrogen-bonding interactions with FAI can more efficiently reduce the lattice disorder and residual 
strain, thereby facilitating the transport of charge carriers. Ultimately, the power conversion efficiency (PCE) of 
devices with 4-ClThPM was increased from 10.20% to 12.10%, and the stability was significantly improved. The 
unencapsulated 4-ClThPM devices maintained 90.24% of the initial PCE after storage in a N2 atmosphere for 
2000 h. This work provides a viable strategy for the multi-site passivation and lattice stabilization of lead-free tin 
perovskite.
