The mesoporous architecture of perovskite solar cells (PSCs) can promote the crystallization of the perovskite 
layer by providing numerous heterogeneous nucleation sites. The morphology of the electron transport layer 
(ETL) and its interface with the perovskite layer are two of the key factors in improving the performance of PSCs. 
While SnO2 is a commonly used ETL material owing to its wide band gap, high transparency and high electron 
mobility. However, the uncontrollable nature of oxygen vacancies in tin oxide during preparation can lead to 
severe carrier recombination at certain sites, thus adversely affecting the open-circuit voltage (Voc) of the device. 
