A B S T R A C T
Perovskite solar cells (PSCs) are currently facing significant challenges in suppressing interfacial defects and 
balancing stability. In this study, we introduce a novel approach by engineering SnO2 electron transport layers 
(ETLs) with 3-amino-4-hydroxybenzenesulfonic acid (3-AS), a conductive small molecule that features dual p-π 
conjugation. The unique structure of 3-AS allows for synergistic π-electron delocalization and the presence of 
multi-functional groups (sulfonic acid, amino, hydroxyl), which together enable the simultaneous passivation of 
defects, modulation of crystallization, and confinement of Pb2⁺. As a result, 3-AS-modified PSCs achieve a 
remarkable power conversion efficiency (PCE) of 25.81 % with significantly reduced hysteresis. This perfor­
mance enhancement is attributed to the decreased trap density and optimized energy alignment at the interfaces. 
More importantly, the modified device retained 91 % of its initial efficiency after 1000 h in high humidity, 
significantly outperforming the control device’s 71 % retention. The optimized device maintained over 92 % 
efficiency after 1600 h of continuous operation in air. Notably, the 3-AS modification reduced lead leakage by 90 
%, and the 3-AS modified device showed Pb2⁺ leaching at ~30 ppb, well below the 50 ppb safety threshold. This 
work not only addresses the critical issues of effi",Electrochimica Acta,Elsevier BV,True,Experimental ETL optimization for single-junction PSCs,False
10.1021/acs.inorgchem.3c01618,Multilateral Passivation Strategy in Post-Synthetic Flexible Metal–Organic Frameworks for Enhancing Perovskite Solar Cells,"The photovoltaic performance of perovskite solar cells is severely limited by the innate defects of perovskite films. Metal–organic framework (MOF)-based additives with luxuriant skeleton structures and tailored functional groups show a huge potential to solve these problems. Here, a multilateral passivation strategy is performed by introducing two alkyl-sulfonic acid functionalized MOFs, MIL-88B-1,3-SO 3 H and MIL-88B-1,4-SO 3 H, respectively, obtained from MIL-88B-NH 2 through a post-synthetic process, for coordinating the lead defects and inhibiting non-radiative recombination. The flexible MIL-88B-type frameworks endow both functionalized MOFs with excellent electrical conductivity and preferable carrier transport in the hole-transport materials. Compared with the original MIL-88B-NH 2 and MIL-88B-1,4-SO 3 H, MIL-88B-1,3-SO 3 H exhibits optimal steric hindrance and multiple passivation groups (−NH 2, −NH–, and −SO 3 H), achieving the champion doped device with an enhanced power conversion efficiency (PCE) of 22.44% and excellent stability, which maintains 92.8% of the original PCE under ambient conditions (40% humidity and 25 °C) for 1200 h.",Inorganic Chemistry,American Chemical Society,True,Experimental passivation additive improves single-junction PSC performance,False
