perovskite solar cells, particularly modules, are far lower than what 
are needed for commercialization2. Great progress has been made 
recently in enhancing the stability of small-area perovskite solar 
cells3–6; however, there were very few studies on improving the sta-
bility of perovskite mini-modules until very recently with extrin-
sic strategies. In one such study, an iodide diffusion barrier was 
designed to suppress mini-module degradation from laser-scribed 
lines7, whereas in another study a holistic approach was developed to 
improve the stability of interfaces in mini-modules8. Nevertheless, 
scalable strategies to improve the intrinsic stability of perovskite 
solar modules with minimal impact on industrial processing are 
still missing.
The composition of perovskites determines their stability 
upper limit—or intrinsic stability—for the encapsulated modules. 
