The prejudicial defects accumulated at the grain boundaries and surface are one of the most important reasons 
that cause perovskite solar cells (PSCs) to deviate from the theoretical Shockley–Queisser efficiency limit and 
poor stability. Consequently, it is highly desirable to reduce defect densities of the polycrystalline perovskite film 
through manipulating perovskite crystallization and introducing passivation strategy. Herein, the synergistic 
effects of cesium trifluoroacetate (Cs-TFA) as a novel additive are demonstrated, where it not only provides 
cesium cations (stabilizer) for perovskite lattice, but also can simultaneously modulate crystal growth and 
passivate grain boundaries defects. The resultant perovskite films possess more regular morphology, larger grain 
sizes, and lower defect density. The enlarged crystal grains derive from the incorporation of Cs-TFA that slows 
down nucleation process, which is demonstrated by in-situ laser confocal photoluminescence imaging. As a 
result, the encouraging efficiency up to 22.82% is realized for the resulting optimized ",Chemical Engineering Journal,Elsevier BV,True,Additive optimization of single-junction PSCs,False
