A B S T R A C T  
Surface modification engineering has been established as a successful method for enhancing the properties of 
organic-inorganic perovskite solar cells (PVK–SCs). In this research, scientists employed guanidine hydrochloride 
(GuHCl) to modify the surface of Cs0.2FA0.8PbI2⋅8Cl0.2 perovskite (PVK:GuHCl) films, leading to a significant 
enhancement in the performance of the devices. After the surface modification, the PVK-SC demonstrated 
notable enhancements in its open-circuit voltage (Voc), which increased from 1.011 V to 1.056 V. Additionally, 
the power conversion efficiency (PCE) experienced a significant boost, surging from 13.1% to an impressive 
18.6%. Additionally, the study highlighted that the reliability of the solar cell was greatly enhanced through 
surface engineering, as evidenced by the packaged device maintained an exceptional 90% of its initial value even 
after being stored at room temperature for six days.   
