A B S T R A C T
The two-step method is a widely utilized technique for the fabrication of high-quality perovskite films for 
perovskite solar cells (PSCs), owing to its ease of operation and enhanced reproducibility. Nevertheless, the dense 
morphology of PbI2 films would lead to an incomplete solid-liquid reaction, thereby compromising the crys­
talline quality of perovskite films. Here, the crystallization kinetics of perovskites are manipulated by introducing 
acetylsalicylic acid (AA) into the PbI2 precursor. The strong interactions between AA and PbI2 result in the 
formation of porous PbI2 layers and promote a sufficient solid-liquid reaction. The high-quality perovskite films 
are obtained with better crystalline quality and decreased defect density and are applied to fabricate high ef­
ficiency inverted flexible PSCs (FPSCs). Meanwhile, the Young's modulus and residual stress are markedly 
diminished, thereby enhancing the mechanical stability of FPSCs. Therefore, the AA-modified FPSCs exhibited a 
high PCE of 23.00 % and excellent stability. The unencapsulated FPSCs can maintain over 85 % of the initial PCE 
after being bent for 10,000 cycles or aging for 1000 h under continuous light soaking (ISOS-L-1).
