E
liminating deleterious defects in semiconductors or pas-
sivating them during the fabrication process of solar cells 
has become one of the most fundamental tasks for the solar 
cell society1–4. This scenario is also prevailing in the metal halide 
perovskite solar cell community, which has witnessed a rapid 
increase of the power conversion efficiency (PCE) of perovskite 
solar cells from 3.8% to 25.5% with overwhelming reported prog-
ress on defect passivation strategies5–8. Many reported perovskite 
solar cells can now pass a 1,000 h operation stability test9,10. Any 
further improvement of the efficiency or stability of perovskite 
solar cells towards their Shockley–Queisser limitations have to rely 
on deeper understandings on the nature of defects in perovskite to 
squeeze out all non-radiative charge recombination paths by elimi-
nating or passivating them. The degradation of solar cells under bias 
or illumination is strongly related to defect evolution11–14. However, 
it is still a challenge to experimentally identify the chemical nature 
of defects in perovskites.
Defects in perovskites have been intensively studied in recent 
years, but there is still no consensus on the defect chemical nature, 
their distribution and their evolution during degradation. Several 
calculations gave controversial results—that is, different defects, 
including halide interstitials (Ii
− and Ii
+), metal vacancies (VPb) or 
antisite (IMA)—that cause deep traps in methylammonium lead tri-
iodide (MAPbI3)15–19. Recent experimental at",Nature Energy,Nature Portfolio,True,Experimental defect study on single-junction PSC degradation,False
