Wide-bandgap (WBG) perovskite solar cells (PSCs) with bandgaps exceeding 1.6 eV have attracted significant 
attention due to their critical role in tandem solar cells. However, wide-bandgap perovskites tend to form high- 
density defects during fabrication, resulting in severe non-radiative recombination, ion migration, and phase 
separation that degrade device efficiency and stability. To address these challenges, we employed interfacial 
engineering to simultaneously suppress bulk and surface defects in perovskite films. Four molecules, α-methyl­
benzylamidodide (MBAI), 4-F-α-methylbenzylamidodide (p-F-MBAI) and their chiral derivatives s-p-F-MBAI and 
r-p-F-MBAI, were introduced at the perovskite/electron transport layer interface. Obviously, p-F-MBAI, with its 
larger dipole moment compared to MBAI, demonstrated superior defect passivation capability, as evidenced by a 
2.3 times enhancement in carrier lifetime relative to MBAI-modified perovskite. Consequently, the optimized 
1.68 eV bandgap PSC achieved a powe",Chemical Engineering Journal,Elsevier BV,True,Experimental defect passivation in single-junction PSCs,False
