ABSTRACT: Perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have demonstrated substantial
development over the past decade; however, this type of device still suffers
from insufficient exploitation stability. In this work, a surface passivation
approach with compact meldonium zwitterions is successfully applied for the
first time. The chemical structure of meldonium zwitterions allows one to
achieve a combined effect on perovskite materials, simultaneously improving
their optoelectronic properties and stability toward various stress factors.
PSCs similarly respond to meldonium passivation via the increase of both
power conversion efficiency and thermo-photostability of encapsulated
devices. A mechanism of hybrid perovskite passivation by meldonium
zwitterions and subsequent stabilization of the light-absorbing material is
suggested in this work for the first time.
