A B S T R A C T
Perovskite solar cells (PSCs) exhibit immense industrialization potential, yet challenges in long-term stability and 
high energy loss impede commercialization. This work introduces a multifunctional interfacial modifier, 4,4’- 
dihydroxyazobenzene-4-carboxylic acid (4-DB), engineered at the SnO2/ perovskite buried interface to concur­
rently address these limitations. The 4-DB molecule serves as: (i) a UV-absorber via trans-cis isomerization, 
converting harmful UV into benign energy; (ii) a conductivity enhancer through extended conjugation and defect 
passivation; and (iii) a crystallization modulator via strong Pb2⁺ coordination. Devices incorporating 4-DB-modi­
fied SnO2 electron transport layers achieve a champion power conversion efficiency (PCE) of 25.60 %, a sig­
nificant enhancement from 23.99 % for control devices. Crucially, unencapsulated 4-DB-modified PSCs retain 
92 % of initial PCE after 1600 h under continuous one sun illumination, outperforming controls. This synergistic 
interface engineering strategy demo",Colloids and Surfaces A: Physicochemical and Engineering Aspects,Elsevier BV,True,experimental PSC interface engineering study,False
