A B S T R A C T
Crystallinity and growth of the perovskite film is a critical factor in high-performance perovskite solar cells 
(PSCs). Ambient humidity is known to influence the crystal growth of the perovskite film during annealing. In 
this study, we employ a scale-up-friendly annealing process on carbon-based PSCs with inorganic hole trans­
porting layer (HTL). The dynamic air flow setup enables utilizing high humidity levels to reconstruct the 
perovskite film and initiate a unidirectional crystalline growth from top to bottom. This process reduces the 
amount of PbI2 secondary phase and increases the thickness of the perovskite layer by approximately 11 %. Our 
results demonstrate that annealing of the perovskite film under an air stream with extreme moisture content 
(about 12 mol%) can highly improve the grain growth and device performance. The humid air flow annealing 
enlarges the grain size by about 50 % and enhances the power conversion efficiency (PCE) from 14.9 % to 17.2 % 
(in average from 14.2 % to 15.7 %), compared to the reference cell. This improvement is mainly achieved by 
around 12 % increase in short-circuit current density (JSC), from 20.7 to 23.2 mA/cm2.
