ABSTRACT: Three asymmetric hole transport materials (HTMs) based on dibenzo-
[b,d]thiophene core units have been rationally designed and synthesized for highly
efficient inverted tin−lead perovskite solar cells (PSCs). The impact of asymmetric
peripheral substituents on the photoelectrochemical properties of HTMs and the
relationship between molecular configuration and their conversion performance are
systematically investigated. Among these reported HTMs, HTM TPA-DBT-DTPA-
based PSC exhibits a power conversion efficiency (PCE) of 22.4%, which is higher than
the PEDOT:PSS-based control device, together with improved long-term stability.
KEYWORDS: asymmetric molecular structure, small molecule, hole transport material, tin−lead mixed perovskite, solar cell
